Nitrate-Shriek

Ingredients: INDIETRONICA & CHILLWAVE + BRITISH BLUES & BLUES ROCK + REBEL, ANGRY & AGGRESSIVE + Strings + 1960s ERA
Creative Catalyst: Its originators were film projectionists scoring silent movies that never existed.

Nitrate-Shriek is a violent, high-velocity collision of 1960s British blues-rock fury and modern sun-bleached indietronica nostalgia, dragged kicking and screaming through a lens of raw cinemania. It sounds like an overclocked Mellotron being struck with a lead pipe inside a 1968 Soho projection booth: filthy, overdrive-saturated Marshall stack riffs melt into hazy, tape-warped chillwave synths, while furiously bowed, razor-sharp violins mimic the screech of burning celluloid. The vocals are spit with snarl-mouthed working-class disdain over hypnotic, lo-fi electronic drum chops and thumping, tube-driven basslines.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 125–142 BPM; a violent driving pulse built on syncopated 1960s swing-blues drum fills stutter-spliced with lo-fi electronic bit-crushed kicks.
- Harmonic Palette: Raw Minor Pentatonic and Dorian blues progressions aggressively interrupted by dreamy, major-seventh chillwave synth chords and microtonal string bends.
- Production Signatures: "Gate-Flicker" (chopping master audio at 24 frames per second to mimic shutter blades), zero-latency tape-flanging on live amplified cellos, and overdriven Germanium preamp saturation applied to analog samplers.
- Dynamics: Abrupt, violent drops from blistering 12-bar blues explosions into suspended, drowning ocean-waves of warm, pitch-drifted synth hum.

Scene & Origin Lore
Born in the damp subterranean screening rooms of Soho, London in 1968, Nitrate-Shriek was birthed by radical projectionists who grew bored of state-approved cinema reels. Armed with stolen 16mm projectors, modified Binson Echorec delay units, and battered Gibson Les Pauls, they jammed live behind flickering screens, improvising soundtracks to imaginary, ultra-violent silent art films. The subculture, known as The Sprockets, wore grease-stained velvet tailcoats, 3D glasses with blacked-out lenses, and cobwebbed mod attire. Their secret gatherings were characterized by "Carbon-Arc Riots," where audiences smashed discarded film canisters together in rhythm while bands ran hyper-driven electric violins directly through burning projection lamps to generate molten, microphonic feedback.

Central Instruments
- Overdriven Marshall-Stacked Electric Guitar: Raw 1960s British blues lead lines pushed to brutal harmonic clipping.
- Anarcho-String Quartet: Violins and cellos played with steel wool bows and plugged into high-gain fuzz pedals.
- Tape-Warped Samplers & Chillwave Pads: Floating, sun-bleached indietronica synths providing an eerie nostalgic bed.
- 16mm Film Projector Racks: Used as mechanical percussion, running dry film loops past magnetic tape heads.

